+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01REPaQ7Q5E1w126CexppVT1","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"My scratch files are removed; `tmp-stress4.ts` was pre-existing, and I left it in place as instructed not to edit.\n\n## Review: Circuit Debugger (commit 8a558e4)\n\n**Verification state:** `npm run build` clean, `npm run lint` clean, `npm test` 21/21 passing in ~0.6s.\n\nI independently stress-tested beyond the committed suite: 60 random 45-bit circuits (0 failures, median 43ms, worst 410ms) and 40 circuits with *locality-focused* swaps confined to single bit positions (0 failures, worst 73ms). The core algorithm is genuinely strong and comfortably inside the 5s budget. `roles.ts` `misnamedGates` produces zero false positives on correct adders at 1/2/3/4/8/45 bits, so its doc claims hold.\n\n### HIGH\n\n**1. Runtime scales with the largest z *index*, not the circuit size — a 2-gate circuit can blow the 5s budget.**\n`/workspace/src/circuit.ts:119` sets `numZ = Math.max(numZ, bit + 1)`, treating the max label as a count. Every tester allocation (`/workspace/src/tester.ts:61`, `:167`) and `firstBadBit`'s loop (`/workspace/src/tester.ts:213`) is sized by `numZ`. Measured on a two-gate circuit whose second output is renamed:\n\n| output wire | time |\n|---|---|\n| `z01` | 0.7 ms |\n| `z9999` | 32 ms |\n| `z99999` | 321 ms |\n| `z999999` | **3118 ms** |\n\nOne mislabeled/adversarial wire name causes a multi-second run on a trivial circuit. The same applies to `numBits` at `/workspace/src/circuit.ts:122`. Recommend deriving `numZ`/`numBits` from contiguous present bits (stop at the first gap), or bounding them by gate count.\n\n**2. `findSwappedWires` can return a result that is not 8 wires, violating the stated contract.**\nThe fallback at `/workspace/src/debugger.ts:46` returns `structuralSuspects(circuit)`, which is `misnamed.slice(0, 8)` — any length 0–8. Confirmed outputs:\n- the suite's own non-adder case (`/workspace/tests/debugger.test.ts:151`) returns `[\"z00\"]` — length 1;\n- a `z00/z01/z09` circuit returns `[\"z01\"]` — length 1.\n\nThe existing test only asserts `Array.isArray(...)`, so this is untested. An odd-length result cannot be interpreted as swap *pairs*. Either document the degenerate contract explicitly or return `[]` when fewer than 8 suspects are found.\n\n### MEDIUM\n\n**3. `simulate` silently produces listing-order-dependent output for double-driven wires.**\n`/workspace/src/simulate.ts:50-61` uses a LIFO `ready` stack and unconditionally overwrites `values.set(gate.out, value)`. With two gates driving `z`, gate order decides the winner — verified: `[AND,OR]` yields `z=0`, `[OR,AND]` yields `z=1` on identical inputs. `compile` rejects this (`/workspace/src/circuit.ts:79`) but `simulate` is exported independently at `/workspace/src/index.ts:2`, so a caller gets a silent wrong answer rather than the error. Recommend the same duplicate-driver check in `simulate`.\n\n**4. The `wasKnown` comment at `/workspace/src/simulate.ts:55` is inaccurate and can drop dependents.**\n\n```ts\nconst wasKnown = values.has(gate.out);\nvalues.set(gate.out, value);\nif (wasKnown) continue; // downstream gates were never blocked on this wire\n```\n\nThe claim holds only when `gate.out` was an *input* wire. If `gate.out` was already written by another gate (case 3), `waiting.get(gate.out)` may hold genuinely blocked dependents whose `missing` counters are then never decremented, silently truncating the result. The comment asserts an invariant the code does not enforce.\n\n**5. `MAX_EVALUATIONS` converts a timeout into a silently wrong answer.**\n`/workspace/src/debugger.ts:90` returns `false` on cap exhaustion, which is indistinguishable from \"no solution\" and falls through to the partial `structuralSuspects` list (issue 2). Given measured worst case is 410ms against a 400k cap, this is unreachable in practice — but if ever hit, the failure is silent. Recommend distinguishing exhaustion from failure, or at minimum documenting that the cap sacrifices correctness.\n\n### LOW\n\n**6. Untracked scratch file left in the repo.** `/workspace/tmp-stress4.ts` is a leftover stress harness (not committed, but present in the working tree). It duplicates coverage now in `/workspace/tests/debugger.test.ts` and should be deleted or folded into the suite.\n\n**7. Register-name regex admits ambiguous aliases.** `/workspace/src/circuit.ts:47` (`/^([xyz])(\\d+)$/`) treats `x0` and `x00` as distinct wires mapping to the same bit 0; the later write wins in `bitsOf` (`:116`). Harmless for well-formed input, worth a note or a fixed-width match.\n\n**8. Unused public surface.** `AdderTester.numTests` (`/workspace/src/tester.ts:29`) is only read internally at `:157`, and `refresh()` (`:106`) is a bare pass-through to `evaluate()`. Minor, but `refresh` adds indirection without adding meaning.\n\n**9. Test generator under-samples the hard case.** `makeSwappedCircuit` (`/workspace/tests/helpers.ts:129-134`) picks 8 gate indices uniformly across all ~222 gates, so swaps are almost always far apart in the carry chain. I had to write a separate generator to exercise same-bit-position swaps. The implementation passed both, but the committed suite alone does not demonstrate that. Recommend adding a locality-constrained generator.\n\n### What was done well\n\nThe decomposition (`circuit` compile → `tester` bit-parallel evaluation → `roles` structural filter → `debugger` guided DFS) is clean and each module has a single clear responsibility. The bit-parallel evaluator packing 128 test vectors into 32-bit lanes (`/workspace/src/tester.ts:190-196`) is the right call for the performance constraint, and the CSR adjacency in `compile` avoids repeated allocation across the search. Deterministic seeding (`mulberry32`) makes results reproducible rather than luck-dependent. The signal-directed repair proposal in `repairsToCarry` genuinely avoids brute-force enumeration as the task required, and the independent `verifier` with a different seed (`/workspace/src/debugger.ts:53`) is a good guard against overfitting to the search vectors."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a3870aa39c847cdda (use SendMessage with to: 'a3870aa39c847cdda' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 56076\ntool_uses: 27\nduration_ms: 212522</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"724ec363-51f3-4528-90cb-67dc3f2d949d","uuid":"cb4af5c3-9c58-4c45-952e-b9029c43ce84","timestamp":"2026-07-25T03:24:23.729Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Review the implementation in /workspace (commit 8a558e4, diff against df4314a — run `git diff df4314a HEAD`).\n\nThe task (see /workspace/TASK.md) was to build:\n1. `simulate(gates, inputs)` — evaluate a logic-gate circuit in dependency order.\n2. `findSwappedWires(gates)` — a ripple-carry adder has exactly 4 pairs of gate output wires swapped; return the 8 wire names sorted. Must handle 45-bit adders (~220 gates) in under 5 seconds; brute-force enumeration is not feasible.\n\nFiles: src/types.ts, src/simulate.ts, src/circuit.ts, src/tester.ts, src/roles.ts, src/debugger.ts, src/index.ts, tests/.\n\nFocus on: correctness bugs, off-by-one errors, edge cases (empty circuits, missing wires, cycles, degenerate inputs), incorrect assumptions in the search algorithm, performance risks, dead code, and clarity. Verify claims made in comments actually hold in the code. Run `npm test`, `npm run build`, `npm run lint` to check the state.\n\nReport findings grouped as HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW priority with file:line references. Do not make any edits — just report.","agentId":"a3870aa39c847cdda","agentType":"conclave:code-reviewer","content":[{"type":"text","text":"My scratch files are removed; `tmp-stress4.ts` was pre-existing, and I left it in place as instructed not to edit.\n\n## Review: Circuit Debugger (commit 8a558e4)\n\n**Verification state:** `npm run build` clean, `npm run lint` clean, `npm test` 21/21 passing 